    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 211102\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 AM MST Fri Aug 21 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Highs will return to around 110 degrees today across the lower \ndeserts and continue into early next week before heating up to \naround 114 degrees during the latter half of next week. \n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings have been extended through next Friday \nevening due to continued areas of major HeatRisk through early next \nweek and increasing to widespread Extreme HeatRisk during the latter \nhalf of next week.\n\n- Isolated thunderstorms are expected to remain confined to the high \nterrain through at least Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nThe subtropical high is currently centered over the Four Corners \nregion early this morning and models are in good agreement that it \nwill slowly progress eastward through tomorrow and eventually settle \nover eastern CO/NM. This will cause winds over our region to become \nmore southerly, which will usher in much drier air. PWATs early this \nmorning currently range from 1.0-1.5\" over our area and models show \nPWATs decreasing to 0.9-1.3\". Models also show mixing ratios as low \nas 7-8 g/kg. This will result in dry conditions across the lower \ndeserts and only slight chances for some isolated showers and storms \nover the higher terrain areas. Models do show slight chances of a \nweak outflow boundary trying to move into the lower deserts. \nHowever, any outflows are expected to wash out as they move off the \nterrain, similar to last night. \n\nDespite the high shifting eastward over the next couple of days, \nmodels show 500mb heights of 593-596dm over our area. Skies will \nreturn to mostly clear by this afternoon, which will allow \ntemperatures to heat back up today and are forecasted to be around \n110-114 (98-108) degrees across the lower deserts (higher terrain) \nthis afternoon. Similar temperatures are forecasted for Saturday and \nmay be a degree or two warmer than today in some locations. These \nhot temperatures will result in widespread Major HeatRisk across the \nlower deserts and in some areas of the higher terrain (specifically \nthe San Carlos area). As a result, the Extreme Heat Warning for the \nlower deserts and San Carlos area remains in effect. Make sure to \npractice smart heat safety by drinking plenty of water and \nelectrolytes and limiting your time outdoors, especially during the \nafternoons.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nModel uncertainty remains quite high for the first half of next week \nas the position of the high is likely to set up somewhere over \neastern New Mexico and western Texas by Monday. This position can \ncause higher uncertainty in moisture levels for our area with the \nlatest guidance trying to advect some moisture into our area by \nSunday or Monday, but NBM forecast PoPs are at most 10-15% for the \nlower deserts. At this point, we could continue to see mostly slight \nchances favoring the high terrain into early next week, but if \nmoisture does eventually improve enough storm chances are likely to \nbe higher than what the NBM is depicting. Another wildcard will be \nthe potential influence of an upper level trough that may attempt to \nmove into western portions of our region Sunday into Monday. If this \noccurs, it may be enough to improve convective potential across \nsoutheast California and western Arizona for Sunday and/or Monday. \nBy Tuesday, drier air will filter back into the region as the \nsubtropical high begins to retrograde back over our region. This \nwill end limit low end rain chances to the higher terrain areas with \nthe lower deserts remaining dry. By the latter half of next week, \nthe NBM shows PoPs being confined to the Mogollon Rim with no \nchances for our CWA. \n\nTemperatures are expected to continue to be more influenced by the \nstrong subtropical high as H5 heights hover between 593-596dm across \nthe Desert Southwest through early next week. Daytime highs are not \nexpected to change much from Sunday to Monday, barring any \nsignificant change with the convective chances. As mentioned above, \nby Tuesday the subtropical high will start to retrograde back to the \nwest and center will eventually settle over AZ/NM during the latter \nhalf of the workweek. The high will also strengthen to around 595-\n597dm during this time. The combination of the center settling over \nour region and strengthening will result in temperatures heating up \nto around 10 degrees above normal for this time of year. Afternoon \nhighs (morning lows) will be in the 110-116 (85-95) degree range \nacross the lower deserts and in the 100-110 (75-85) degree range \nacross the higher terrain areas. The increase in temperatures will \nalso result in an increase in HeatRisk, with widespread Extreme \nHeatRisk forecasted for the majority of the CWA for the latter half \nof the workweek. Due to these hot temperatures and widespread \nExtreme HeatRisk expected next week, the Extreme Heat Warning has \nbeen extended through next Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1100Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major weather issues will exist through Saturday morning under \noccasional mid/high cloud decks. Confidence is good that winds will \ncomplete the switch to W/SW early/mid afternoon with minimal \ngustiness. While TSRA will not directly affect any terminal, a \ndistant traveling outflow could switch winds to S/SE this evening, \nhowever confidence is too low to include in this TAF package. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather issues will exist through Saturday morning under \nmostly clear skies. Winds will generally oscillate between SE and SW \nwith limited afternoon/evening gusts, however thunderstorm outflow \nwinds may create an early wind switch to SW at KIPL late this \nafternoon. Otherwise, periods of variable winds will be common \nduring the early morning hours. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air will filter into the region today and Saturday with even \nlower chances for rain while temperatures heat back up to 5 to 8 \ndegrees above normal. Only slight chances for isolated storms exist \nover the higher terrain through Saturday. MinRHs of 15-25% today \ndecrease to 12-20% tomorrow. MinRh then increases again to 15-25% \nSunday and Monday before decreasing back to 10-20% beginning \nTuesday. Overnight recovery will generally be in the 30-60% range, \nwith the best night being Sunday night. Winds should overall follow \nthe typical diurnal tendencies with some modest afternoon upslope \ngustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 21   114 in 2019    115 in 1969    117 in 1969\nAug 22   113 in 2011    115 in 1969    117 in 1969\nAug 23   114 in 2011    115 in 2011    116 in 1969\nAug 24   115 in 2020    119 in 1985    117 in 1985\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530>544-546-\n     548>551-553>555-559-560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich/Kuhlman \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich\nCLIMATE...18\n",
